reports.click-4f1302f13075:
I wanted to share a custom argument class between several commands, so I built the decorator once and applied it to more than one command:

```python
my_arg = click.argument("name", cls=MyArgument)

@click.command()
@my_arg
def first(name): ...

@click.command()
@my_arg
def second(name): ...
```

I expected both commands to use my custom argument class, but only the first one does — the second command ends up with a plain `click.Argument` instead. The same thing happens with `click.option(..., cls=...)` when the decorator is reused, and it's confusing because nothing warns me that the decorator can only be applied once.

reports.click-6017fe42f482:
I have a command-line flag whose value is set with `flag_value`, and I also gave it an `envvar` so it can be turned on from the environment. When I set that environment variable to anything at all, the option's value comes out as the raw text of the environment variable instead of the flag value I configured. I expected the environment variable to just switch the flag on, so the value would always be `upper` no matter what the variable contains.

```python
@click.command()
@click.option("--upper", flag_value="upper", envvar="UPPER")
def cmd(upper):
    click.echo(upper)
```

Running `UPPER=whatever cmd` prints `whatever`; I expected it to print `upper`.

reports.click-679517d3ee49:
I have a repeatable option that can be given with or without a value, so I set `is_flag=False` and `flag_value`. When I pass it several times and leave the value off some of those occurrences, those bare occurrences don't come back as my flag value — I get a meaningless placeholder object in the result instead.

```python
@click.command()
@click.option("-f", is_flag=False, flag_value="flag", multiple=True)
def cli(f):
    click.echo(f)
```

Running `cli -f -f other -f` prints `(<object object at 0x...>, 'other', <object object at 0x...>)`, but I expected `('flag', 'other', 'flag')`. Giving the option just once without a value works fine, so it seems to break only when it's repeated and mixed with values.

reports.click-893d8aa42974:
I have a command with a boolean flag that I marked as required:

```python
import click

@click.command()
@click.option("--on/--off", is_flag=True, required=True)
def cli(on):
    click.echo(on)
```

I expected that running `cli` with no arguments would stop with an error telling me the option is missing, since I declared it required. Instead the command runs anyway and prints `False`, so the required setting seems to be ignored for this flag.

reports.click-c851620935ec:
I use a default map to turn a boolean flag on by default, but the help text doesn't reflect it. With this command:

```python
@click.command()
@click.option("--name/--no-name", is_flag=True, show_default=True)
def foo(name):
    click.echo(name)
```

running `foo` with `default_map={"foo": {"name": True}}` correctly prints `True`, yet `foo --help` still says `default: no-name`. I expected the help to say `default: name`, matching the value the flag actually takes. The help text seems to ignore the default map and only look at the flag's own default.

reports.click-e5fa0258b14c:
I have a command with an option that reads its value from an environment variable, and I want an empty value to be treated as if the variable were not set at all. Instead, when the variable is present but empty, the empty string is passed through as the option's value, which breaks validation:

```python
@click.command()
@click.option("--mypath", type=click.Path(exists=True), envvar="MYPATH")
def cli(mypath):
    click.echo(f"mypath: {mypath}")
```

Running this with `MYPATH=""` in the environment gives `Error: Invalid value for '--mypath': Path '' does not exist.` and a non-zero exit code, where I expected `mypath: None` and a clean exit. The same thing happens when the variable comes from the automatic prefix instead of an explicit `envvar`.

reports.click-ffc928bfe723:
I have a CLI with a command whose name contains a colon, and I gave that command a help string. When I use zsh completion, tab-completing that command inserts only the part before the colon instead of the full command name. For example:

```python
@click.group()
def cli():
    pass

@cli.command("c:d", help="cee:dee")
def c_d():
    pass
```

Typing `cli c` and pressing Tab completes to `c`, not `c:d`. If I remove the help string it works, but with the help text the command is cut off at the first colon; I expected the full name `c:d` to be completed.
